The bar model shows the ratio $2:3$.
Write two ratios that are equivalent to $2:3$.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"ratioBlueFill","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Shaded parts distinguish the first ratio group from the second."}],"reference":"Existing group counts and geometry retained; standard palette; ordinary outlines black"}
\definecolor{ratioBlueFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (0,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (1,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (2,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (3,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (4,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"ratioBlueFill","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Shaded parts distinguish the first ratio group from the second."}],"reference":"Existing group counts and geometry retained; standard palette; ordinary outlines black"}
\definecolor{ratioBlueFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (0,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (1,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (2,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=ratioBlueFill] (3,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (4,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (5,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (6,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (7,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (8,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\draw[fill=white] (9,0) rectangle ++(1,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$2:3=(2\times2):(3\times2)=4:6$
$2:3=(2\times3):(3\times3)=6:9$
